# Poetry Slam - Barnstable High School Auditorium

A "Slammer" takes the stage

@ Slammer

> If I must become a military mime where mum is the word until the end of time, then so be it, but then heed my final rhymes.

> You do not want to go to war with me, for that war would last eternity.

> In the beginning as the end, life is where our time is spent.

> Now I kindly stepped outside and asked around, "Where does every body's time abound?"

> And for the end of my chagrin, they peeled their curtains back and let me in.

> One by one they showed me where, way back then they met a clown back there.

> "Why does every body point me clown?", and they say that "It's your frown."

> To prove them right I did magic, so they'd smirk, "I knew you had it!"

> Our paths diverge and we part ways, I say "See you at The End of Days."

> That is the tent at which we circus, for juggling is where the work is.

> As each day ends and night begins, that's the cue to seek yourself therein.

> "If I must become a military mime where mum is the word until the end of time, then so be it, but then heed my final rhymes."

# Cape Cod Times - HQ

A phone rings on a desk in a fancy office, the fanciest one in the building

@ Reporter
> Hello.

The REPORTER listens and facially reacts to this inaudible dialogue

@ Police Chief (I.A.) 
> A group of high schoolers has just been arrested for invading the Melody Tent after parading themselves out of a Silicon Valley Engineer and Hollywood Producer's slam poem on the risks of "Artificial Intelligence" at the High School on West Main Street. I just want to give you heads up that the poet advocates for tearing down The Sturgis Charter School to put the Amusement Center back on what he started calling "East Main Street" to solve the fenty epidemic and his reasoning is pretty sound and we have nothing to hold him on, so we released him and he is on his way to your office now to pitch you on his latest immersive experience based on what he said he learned doing what he loved becoming: "The Clown Mayor of San Francisco".

A knock on the door

@ Ty
> Have you ever read Player Piano?

Ty hands the reporter his annotated copy of Kurt Vonnegut's debut novel.

